+# Ordinary byte-publication fixture
+
+`transport-artifact.json` is a frozen transport-only `mori_backend::Artifact`
+constructor input for the file-publication tests in `mori/src/commands/compile.rs`.
+Its opaque publication bytes are **not compiled ZKIR**, native-valid artifacts,
+or financial/ledger evidence. The two array entries are file-order specimens,
+not the two positive U2 program walkthroughs. No source program is supplied.
+
+The falsifiable predicate is: publication writes each supplied UTF-8 string
+unchanged to its indexed filename, preserves array order, and writes the exact
+supplied manifest bytes last as the completion marker. A provided projection
+is another exact-byte leaf before that marker. Artifact decoding is only the
+constructor path; successful decoding does not certify the supplied payloads.
+
+This contract was frozen independently from the public schema and existing
+publication assertions at baseline
+`baf61ebd0cb49f2c4441cb7fa8de53b343e056dc`. No implementation candidate or
+compiler output was consulted. No compiler, evaluator, Cargo, rustc, model,
+SDK, VM, key, signing, proof, ledger, network or private cache/capture action
+was used to produce these values. Standard-library JSON encoding and SHA-256
+were used only to package and identify these literal transport bytes.
+
+## Frozen input and expected bytes
+
+The JSON file is UTF-8, indented with two spaces, and has **no terminal newline**.
+Its literal file size is 2424 bytes; SHA-256 is
+`4e66f47b2c055deaa595290c6989554364d7520c2ba957e03669608b6c783447`. Pass these exact bytes as the manifest string when testing
+the frozen manifest contract. A test may decode using
+`serde_json::from_str::<mori_backend::Artifact>`; it must not replace the public
+decoder or compile a source program to construct this artifact.
+
+The circuit array is exactly `z_first`, then `a_second`, intentionally opposite
+to lexicographic action ordering. Indexing starts at zero and uses three digits.
+The following escaped strings denote the complete leaf payloads; `\n` means
+one LF byte, and each Greek letter is its literal UTF-8 encoding:
+
+```text
+publication fixture: z_first\nopaque UTF-8: α\n
+publication fixture: a_second\nopaque UTF-8: β\n
+```
+
+The letters are `α` = `ce b1` and `β` = `ce b2` in hexadecimal. Both payloads
+end in LF; there is also one LF between their two lines. Neither has a CR,
+BOM, extra final blank line, or JSON escaping in the published leaf.
+
+| Published filename | Byte count | SHA-256 |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| `000-z_first.zkir` | 46 | `c1a64f94f4cef172b10175423186f2d41b29f6a4986629d92a7dd2f974880904` |
+| `001-a_second.zkir` | 47 | `857aaff9ede141a511b448ad466145c7fd13aec7a4666d21f34030ef990bc5c7` |
+| `artifact.json` | 2424 | `4e66f47b2c055deaa595290c6989554364d7520c2ba957e03669608b6c783447` |
+
+With no projection, a fresh reserved output directory has exactly these three
+files on success. `source-projection.json` is absent. `artifact.json` equals
+the supplied manifest string byte for byte; the publisher must not parse,
+normalize, or add a newline to it.
+
+For the positive projection case, supply the exact Rust string literal
+`"{\"transport_fixture\":\"projection\"}\n"`. Its decoded bytes are
+`{"transport_fixture":"projection"}` followed by one LF, totaling
+35 bytes; SHA-256 is `c826a58ecde287b2742ff1606b98dcf08c439185df611e24f3d502fb91b76474`.
+Success has exactly four files: the same two ordered circuit leaves,
+`source-projection.json` containing those 35 bytes unchanged, and
+`artifact.json` containing the same frozen manifest. The projection is a
+transport specimen, not an actual source hash or compilation report.
+
+## Completion and existing refusal assertions
+
+The required publication sequence is `000-z_first.zkir`,
+`001-a_second.zkir`, optional `source-projection.json`, then `artifact.json`.
+The marker may appear only after all requested non-marker leaves are complete.
+A pre-existing marker alone is never evidence of completion by this attempt.
+
+For the existing regular-file refusal cases without projection, seed the
+collision leaf with `third-party sentinel` and an unrelated file named
+`unrelated` with `user addition`. Both byte strings have no newline and must
+remain unchanged. Refusal retains the completed prefix and writes no later
+leaf; these independently expected totals include the two seeded entries:
+
+| Collision leaf | Complete new circuit prefix | Final directory entry count |
+| --- | --- | ---: |
+| `000-z_first.zkir` | none | 2 |
+| `001-a_second.zkir` | `000-z_first.zkir` | 3 |
+| `artifact.json` | both circuit leaves | 4 |
+
+For a collision on either circuit, the completion marker must remain absent.
+For a collision on the marker, its original sentinel bytes remain unchanged.
+
+The existing Unix link/directory cases seed only a collision at the second
+circuit or marker, without `unrelated`: expected counts are respectively two
+and three. Preserve the original symlink target or directory, an existing
+external target's `external sentinel` bytes, and the absence of a dangling
+link's external target. In all such cases the first circuit bytes are exactly
+the frozen `z_first` payload. The second circuit collision leaves no marker.
+These are the retained publication assertions, not a claim that hostile or
+security validation was performed by this fixture owner.
+
+## Why the public decoder can construct it
+
+`mori-backend/src/lib.rs` declares every included `Artifact`, `CircuitArtifact`,
+`Resources` and `Target` field. The version decoders require exactly
+`moriarty-zkir-artifact/3`, `moriarty-public-abi/3` and
+`moriarty-zkir-relation/3`. Unsigned mode matches the unsigned target because
+`signed_policy` is omitted; explicit null is intentionally not supplied.
+The target has the exact pinned `ledger-9` profile, ledger revision
+`04c9c5d9bcebb8d4427d8589fb54d58a55599c14`, and ZKIR version `3.0.0-rc.2`.
+`local` and `publication-fixture` are nonempty bounded labels; the limits are
+positive integers, 100000 instructions and 16777216 artifact bytes.
+They identify this local transport specimen, not an observed network target.
+
+The program digest is exactly 64 `0` characters; circuit context digests are
+exactly 64 `1` characters and 64 `2` characters in array order. They satisfy
+the lowercase 32-byte hexadecimal decoding shape only. They are deliberately
+literal placeholders, not recomputed program/context commitments. Context
+constants likewise are descriptor-shape literals, not digest limb claims.
+
+Each circuit has two public inputs with role `context`, names
+`program_target_action.0` and `program_target_action.1`, type `UInt`, explicit
+null `asset`, and canonical decimal `constant` strings (`0`, `1` for the first;
+`2`, `3` for the second). This satisfies the unsigned branch of
+`signed_relation::validate_shape`: authorization and private-input arrays are
+empty. Effect, balance and liability frames are empty; replay steps and
+instruction counts are zero. No Type, AssetId or descriptor is inferred from
+a compiler or evaluator.
+
+Resources have `instructions: 0`, `zkir_bytes: 93`,
+`artifact_bytes: 2424`, `public_values: 4`, `max_replay_steps: 0`,
+explicit null `circuit_k` and `fraud_max_k`, `cost_measured: false`,
+`liability_registry_entries: 0`, and `max_liability_registry_entries: 16`.
+The registry decoders permit zero entries and require the pinned ceiling 16.
+The byte counters describe this packaged fixture only. Empty `gates` asserts
+no completed gate, and `moriarty-core/1` is the literal semantics label.
+
+The constructor checks the versions, target, mode, digest encoding and
+unsigned descriptor shape. This constructor assessment comes from source
+inspection; Rust serde decoding and publication tests were not run by the
+fixture owner. It does not parse the `zkir` strings or establish
+cryptographic validity of the placeholder digests/context. Do not send this
+fixture to native validation or treat its schema acceptance as a compiled
+artifact result. If a later decoder adds a requirement incompatible with
+opaque strings, report that exact field or predicate; use an explicitly typed
+transport-test construction if appropriate instead of bypassing or weakening
+the production decoder.
